<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis VIII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1187">1187</a>–<a href="/years/1226">1226</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Lion · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis VIII, called the Lion, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1223 to 1226 and son of Philip II Augustus. As prince he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1216 at the invitation of rebel barons but was defeated at Lincoln in 1217. As king he completed the conquest of Poitou from the English in 1224 and led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Albigensians</a>. He began the practice of granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> to royal sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VIII of France" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Natalis de Wailly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born on 5 September 1187, the son of King Philip II Augustus and Isabella of Hainault. His mother died in 1190, and his father allowed him nominal control of the county of Artois to learn governance. On 23 May 1200 he married Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of Castile, after prolonged negotiations with her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">John, King of England</a>. In 1214 Philip faced an alliance that included John, and Louis was given command against John in Poitou. After losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> he forced John to retreat from the castle of Roche-au-Moine, while Philip won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1215 Louis, fulfilling his father's crusading vow, went against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Albigensians</a> and favoured Simon de Montfort the Elder at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, ordering the walls of both to be pulled down. That year English barons rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> and offered the throne to Louis, whose wife descended from Henry II. He landed in Kent on 21 May 1216 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> unopposed, where he was proclaimed king though never crowned. The papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guala-bicchieri">Guala Bicchieri</a> excommunicated him on 29 May.</p><p>John's death in October 1216 led many barons to desert Louis for John's son, Henry III. Louis's army was beaten at the Battle of Lincoln on 20 May 1217 and his fleet at the Battle of Sandwich on 24 August. By the Treaty of Lambeth he pledged never to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> again, English rebels were given an amnesty, he received 10,000 marks and his excommunication was lifted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Louis succeeded his father on 14 July 1223 and was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 6 August. On 1 November 1223 he issued an ordinance forbidding his officials to record debts owed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, reversing his father's policy; the financial effect was so great that in 1225 he invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> moneylenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In 1224 he refused to renew the truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and took Poitou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, leaving the English kings only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> on the continent.</p><p>In 1226 he took the cross against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Albigensians</a> and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaucaire">Beaucaire</a> and Marseille. Avignon held out until 9 September, at heavy cost, and his weakened army did not attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. He died of dysentery on 8 November 1226 at the Château de Montpensier. His son Louis IX succeeded him, and his younger sons received apanages, among them Artois, Anjou and Maine, and Poitou and Auvergne. He is buried at the Saint Denis Basilica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis VIII of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isabella of Hainaut, daughter of Baldwin V, married Philip II Augustus of France in 1180 and was the mother of <b>Louis VIII</b>, so later French kings descended from the counts of Hainaut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The intervention of the French crown proved decisive: <b>Louis VIII</b>’s crusade of 1226 broke resistance, and by the Treaty of Paris of 1229 Raymond VII kept a reduced county for life, while his only daughter Joan was married to Alphonse of Poitiers, brother of Louis IX.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The French crown conquered Poitou between 1204 and 1224, when <b>Louis VIII</b> took La Rochelle, and Henry III's attempt to recover it failed at the Battle of Taillebourg in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The last count, William, bishop of Châlons, died in 1226, and <b>Louis VIII</b> and Louis IX absorbed the county into the royal domain of the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perche">County of Perche</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II seized Poitou from King John from 1204, and <b>Louis VIII</b> completed the conquest in 1224.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-poitou">County of Poitou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Montpensier, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1212" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis VIII of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1212">What was here in 1212? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
